## Handling Both Paths with Match

Every Result operation has two possible outcomes. Match lets you handle each one with dedicated logic.

### Basic Result Matching

```xiom
fn check_number(n: Int) -> Result[Int, Str] {
  if n > 0 { return Ok(n); }
  return Err("Number must be positive");
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let r1 = check_number(42);
  let r2 = check_number(-5);

  match r1 {
    Ok(val) => io.println("Valid: " + string.int_to_str(val)),
    Err(msg) => io.println("Error: " + msg),
  };

  match r2 {
    Ok(val) => io.println("Valid: " + string.int_to_str(val)),
    Err(msg) => io.println("Error: " + msg),
  };
  return 0;
}
```

### Different Actions per Outcome

```xiom
fn process_login(result: Result[Str, Str]) {
  match result {
    Ok(token) => {
      io.println("Login successful!");
      io.println("Token: " + token);
    },
    Err(reason) => {
      io.println("Login failed.");
      io.println("Reason: " + reason);
      io.println("Please try again.");
    },
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  process_login(Ok("abc123"));
  process_login(Err("Wrong password"));
  return 0;
}
```

### Transforming Results

Use match to convert a Result into a different value:

```xiom
fn get_grade_label(result: Result[Int, Str]) -> Str {
  return match result {
    Ok(score) => {
      if score >= 90 { return "Grade: A"; }
      if score >= 80 { return "Grade: B"; }
      return "Grade: C";
    },
    Err(e) => "Could not calculate: " + e,
  };
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(get_grade_label(Ok(95)));   // Grade: A
  io.println(get_grade_label(Ok(72)));   // Grade: C
  io.println(get_grade_label(Err("No data")));  // Could not calculate: No data
  return 0;
}
```

